paired, it serves as the required pair. If it becomes a Pung (by drawing two more), the hand upgrades to **Little Three Dragons** (64 points) or **Big Three Dragons** (88 points). Keeping J3 has high potential upside and fits the pair requirement.\n- **T7, T8, T9:** Forms a valid Chow. Keep.\n- **T1, T3:** These are isolated/incomplete shapes. T1-T3 is an edge interval waiting for T2.\n    - If T1 and T3 are kept and T2 is drawn, the player forms T1-T2-T3 (Meld). The hand would then have 5 melds (J2, B4, J1, T123, T789) and **no pair**, which is not a winning hand structure.\n    - Therefore, to ensure a pair exists, the player should discard the T1-T3 shape and focus on pairing J3 (or T3).\n    - Comparing T1 and T3: T3 is more useful as it connects to both T2 and T4, whereas T1 only connects to T2. T1 is the least versatile tile.\n\nEvaluation: The hand is aiming for an 8-point minimum via \"Two Dragon Pungs\" (6), \"Melded Kong\" (1), and \"One Voided Suit\" (1). The J1 pair must be upgraded to a Pung, requiring the formation of a new pair elsewhere. J3 is the ideal candidate for this pair due to its high-scoring potential (Three Dragons). T1 and T3 are superfluous and threaten the hand structure by potentially forming a 5th meld instead of a pair. T1 is less useful than T3 due to limited connectivity.\n\nReason: To secure the 8-point minimum, the J1 pair must become a Pung, which necessitates finding a new pair. J3 is retained for this purpose and for the chance at \"Big/Little Three Dragons\". T1 and T3 are not needed; T1 is discarded as it has strictly worse connectivity (only T2) compared to T3 (T2 and T4).\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
